-- A late fee of 2 for each day a tool comes back late. Reference §10 "Dates and durations" says
-- "`done - planned` on two `Date` values is a whole number of days", so `returnedOn - dueOn` is
-- the number of days late. `velle validate` refuses it:
--
--   - [F2] 'Return.daysLate' is declared an integer and computed as a duration; declare it 'daysLate: duration', or compute an integer
--
-- The compiler reads two dates subtracted as a duration, not a whole number, and nothing turns a
-- duration into a number. Each other spelling tried is refused too, with `daysLate` declared
-- as below:
--
--   `daysLate: integer = (returnedOn - dueOn) / 1 days`
--   - [F2] 'Return.daysLate' — the compiler reads no type off '(returnedOn - dueOn) / 1 days' to check against the declared integer
--   `lateFee: decimal = 2 * (returnedOn - dueOn)`
--   - [F2] 'Return.lateFee' — the compiler reads no type off '2 * (returnedOn - dueOn)' to check against the declared decimal
--   `daysLate: integer = days(returnedOn - dueOn)`
--   - [F2] 'days(returnedOn - dueOn)': no builtin or registered function is named 'days' (Reference §5)
--   `daysLate: integer = (returnedOn - dueOn).days`
--   - [F2] 'Return.daysLate' — the compiler reads no type off '(returnedOn - dueOn).days' to check against the declared integer
--
-- The first message offers `daysLate: duration`, which no fee can be multiplied by. None of the
-- messages says that no spelling gives a number of days. `38-days-between-dates-workaround.velle`
-- is what an author can write today.
act Return {
    dueOn: Date
    returnedOn: Date
    daysLate: integer = returnedOn - dueOn
    lateFee: decimal = 2 * daysLate
}
